So to give you some of his stunts, he first really came to public attention in 1956. He rescued a 15-year-old Catholic girl called Morrill Lions. More Allian's had decided she wanted to join Ian Pais' church and her parents forbade it. So he and his reporters smuggled her to Scotland. You know, this was a massive story. I mean, they basically kidnapped this teenager. And he was absolutely unrepentant. He called a rally. Whenever he's in trouble, he calls a rally in the Ulster Hall in Belfast. And he said, they're saying, I'm going to have to go to prison. I would be proud to do time for Protestant liberty. Like he always says this kind of thing. That was actually Richard Nixon's voice.

117
01:01:30.000 --> 01:02:03.000
Two years later, the queen, he condemns, I mean, he loves putting the boot into the British when they let the side down. The queen mother and Princess Margaret made a courtesy call to Pope John the 23rd. He said of them, they've been committing spiritual fornication and adultery with the Antichrist. I mean, accusing the Queen mother of fornication and adultery is strong stuff, I think, especially with the Antichrist. I mean, it would get you in the papers. wouldn't it? It would. Saying stuff like that.

118
01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:27.000
Or when the Pope died in 1963, Terrence O'Neill, a consulatory gesture, said we will lower the Union Jack as a mark of respect, because after all, one in three of our citizens is actually a Catholic. Paisley went absolutely berserk at this. He said, I can't stand the lying euloges now being paid to the Roman Antichrist by the Escarats of Ulster. He said, the good news, though, he said,

119
01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:49.000
the Romish man of sin is now in hell. Well, that's something. Do you remember in the 80s he disrupted the European Parliament? Yeah, he did. There was lots about the Antichrist and the Hall of Babylon and stuff. He was an MEP at this point, wasn't he? Was he not? And the Pope, Pope John Paul II, came to address the European Parliament,

120
01:02:49.000 --> 01:03:25.000
and Ian Paisley started, that was bundled out of the hall, shouting, I denounce you as Antichrist. He had massive jowls by that point, and they would kind of wobble all over his chest. Yeah. Well, anyway, we come to the Westminster General Election of 1964, and this story, this really is the politics of Northern Ireland in microcosm. So we're in West Belfast, which is historically over the city's four seats, the most competitive, because it contains the heavily Catholic Falls Road, which you mentioned earlier. And one of the candidates was a guy called Liam McMillan, who was an IRA man.

121
01:03:25.000 --> 01:04:03.000
He'd been active in the border campaign, and he is standing as an independent Republican. And in the window of his election office in the lower falls, he hangs this big Irish tricolour flag. Now, under the Stormont Flags and Emblems Act of 1954, the RUC are empowered to take down any flags that they think are likely to provoke a breach of the peace. And this legislation was aimed specifically at people flying the Irish flag. But the police aren't completely stupid. When people fly it in Catholic areas, they generally leave it alone. just let them get on with it if that's what they want to do. Paisley is horrified by this.

122
01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:24.000
He calls a big rally and he says, I do not accept that any part of Ulster is Republican. I do not want to see the tricolor flying here. And under this pressure, the IUC decide fine, we'll take it down. So on the 28th of September, a group of heavily armed police constables smashed down the door of this bloke's office and they carry off the flag

123
01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:46.000
through a crowd of 2,000 people who were sort of jeering at them and throwing stones at them and stuff. And this kicked off the worst rioting in Ulster since the 1930s. In some ways, you could say this is a possible departure point for the troubles. There are thousands of people throwing stones and petrol bombs, people set a trolley bus on fire, and it ends with hundreds of our UC men,

124
01:04:46.000 --> 01:05:16.000
armed with batons, supported by armored cars and water cannons, physically clearing the falls road. And 50 people end up in hospital, as well as 18, RUC constables. And there's also copycat rioting, or sort of tip-for-tat rioting, by the way, in Dublin. There's a crowd when they hear this that assemble outside the British embassy, and they have to be held back by the Irish police, and they're kind of throwing stones of them. And this is the first point, really, since the war, that the British papers almost

125
01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:41.000
noticed Northern Ireland. So the Times, there is a sickening familiarity about all of this, it says, let's hope we're not returning to the bitterness and savagery of the orange and green vendetta. But from Paisley's point of view, this has been very effective. It's got his name in the newspapers once again. He's made a stand. The Irish flag has come down. He is the champion of Protestant liberty, as he would call it.

126
01:05:41.000 --> 01:06:19.000
And two weeks later, when the unionist candidate Jim Kilfeeder wins West Belfast, he makes a point in his speech of thanking Ian Paisley for his support. So that sets the scene for two years in which Ian Paisley is becoming ever more strident in his attacks on what he sees as the soft, weak, English, untrustworthy, Terrence O'Neill. So the visit of Sean Lamass from Dublin. This is when Ian Pace is going around shouting, no mass, no Lamass. And then he gets an even better gift a year later. It's going to be the 50th anniversary of the Easter Rising.

127
01:06:19.000 --> 01:06:50.000
And you said yourself, you know, on the Falls Road, there are all the murals to the stay of the Easter Rising. They make a big deal of it. Of course, 1916, if you're a unionist, it's the year of the Somme. So it's like a history war, I guess, being played out in the streets of Belfast. O'Neill says, fine, let the Catholics have a, if they want to have a march to celebrate the Easter rising 50 years on, of course we'll let them do it. You know, it will be mad not to it. It will just create great tension if we ban it.

128
01:06:50.000 --> 01:07:17.000
Paisley is horrified by this. He tells a rally that by allowing this nationalist march, O'Neill is supporting murder, treason, sedition. Our forefathers suffered from bullets and bombs and won, we will do the same. Ian Pacey loves very violent rhetoric. Then there's another confrontation, June 1966. This time he's attacking the mainstream churches. So the mainstream Presbyterian church are holding their annual assembly.

129
01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:45.000
Paisley decides to lead 1,500 supporters to attack them for their Romanizing tendencies, as he quotes it. basically you could never be hard enough on Catholicism, I think for the Reverend Ian Paisley. And he leads his supporters through this very Catholic area deliberately. They're attacked by local youths. And the melee ends with lots of stone throwing, fighting, people throwing bricks at the IUC, all of this kind of thing.

130
01:07:45.000 --> 01:08:24.000
The magistrates then order Paisley to sign a court order to keep the peace, a pledge to keep the peace. And he says, no, I'm not going to do that. and they sentenced him to three months in Crumlin Road prison, and that provoked yet more rioting, thousands of his supporters going on the rampage, chanting his name, people attacking Catholic-owned businesses, Catholic shops, and so on and so forth. At this point, Terrence O'Neill, the Prime Minister of Northern Ireland, says, okay, enough, I've lost patience. I'm banning all marches, Protestant or Catholic, for the next three months. The RUC have the power to shut down all meetings,

131
01:08:24.000 --> 01:08:47.000
with more than three people in. You know, no more of this. And he gives a very, very outspoken speech at Stormont. And he says, Paisley and his supporters call themselves loyalists. But to whom are they loyal? To the Queen, whose personal representatives they revile. To the United Kingdom in which their fellow citizens view their conduct with a mixture of ridicule and contempt.

132
01:08:47.000 --> 01:09:12.000
To Protestantism, many of whose leaders they have personally abused. I mean, it's good stuff. But O'Neill all the time is losing momentum And it's Paisley who is attracting momentum And part of this is the sort of ratchet effect of sectarian rhetoric But it's also the economic context The fact that people feel anxious about the future The fact that Northern Ireland feels like it's a changing society

133
01:09:12.000 --> 01:09:30.000
And a lot of people don't like that All of these kinds of things And all the time Paisley is attracting more recruits for his church And for its political wing which was called Ulster Protestant Action. He had set this up twofold reasons, really. One, he'd said this will deter the IRA, but he also said,

134
01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:50.000
we want to keep loyal workers in employment in times of deep depression. So there's an economic context of this too. But this is not the only group that he's associated with. There's a whole series of a bewildering variety of factions and subgroups and whatnot. So I mentioned that he had a protégé called Noel Doherty. In April

135
01:09:50.000 --> 01:09:59.000
1966 with the anniversary of the Easter rising coming Docherty set up another group a vigilante group called the Ulster Protestant volunteers

136
01:09:59.000 --> 01:10:08.000
The stated name of this group is we think the IRA going to launch an uprising on the anniversary of the Easter rising and we're going to be ready for them we'll have cells all over Alster

137
01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:21.000
and they will rise up on command the name the Ulster Protestant volunteers obviously is to evoke Carson's volunteers from 1912 the motto is the same for God and Ulster.

138
01:10:21.000 --> 01:10:45.000
And like Carson's volunteers, they really want to get a hold of guns. So Doherty recruits very heavily among the B specials, the kind of police reservists, because they have been issued with revolvers and submachine guns, and they're allowed to keep their weapons at home. So this is a very useful thing for a paramilitary group. And Docti said later, my aim was to have a private army just like Carson did

139
01:10:45.000 --> 01:11:18.000
with the signing of the covenant. So Paisley would have had his own, a private army and he would have been a second Lord Carson. Can I ask Dominic, would the Protestants in Ulster at this point be ahead of the Catholics in terms of army? Well, given how many guns that be specials have, I would say probably yes. And actually when the IRA re-enter the story, let's say 1970, they don't have that many guns. And getting guns, armolites as they end up getting, becomes a real priority for them. Because of course, they've made the cardinal error, which you should never do, giving enough guns to the Welsh.

140
01:11:18.000 --> 01:11:51.000
Yes, of course. And the Welsh don't want to give them back. Have you ever tried to reclaim a gun from a Welshman? I can't say I have. It's impossible. It can't be done. Okay. Well, that is a mistake then. Yeah. So anyway, Noel Doherty, this guy, is going round in the spring of 1966 trying to get guns and jet ignite. And he comes into contact at this point with another loyalist vigilante group that also looks back to Carson's volunteers. and this is a group called the Ulster Volunteer Force, the UVF.

141
01:11:51.000 --> 01:12:26.000
And it's been founded a few months earlier by a group of men who used to meet in the pubs in the working class Protestant Shankill area. And the chief among them, and a big figure in the story of the troubles, although he's not so well known in Britain as some of the other figures, it's a man called Gusty Spence, who was in his early 30s. Spence's father had been Carson's volunteers, Spencer's father had also served in the First World War, Spence himself had been in the British Army. He'd been in Cyprus. He came home to Belfast. He worked as a scaffolder of the Harland and Wolf shipyards.

142
01:12:26.000 --> 01:12:48.000
So he sort of ticks all of these different boxes. Spence is a very hard man. He's no stranger to a brawl in a pub or something. He's a very keen member of the Orange Order. People look up to him because he's been in the army. There's a bit of an element, I think, about him and his friends of the old soldiers who are hanging around in the pub, talking about the old days,

143
01:12:48.000 --> 01:13:02.000
trying to find something to do. They want to cause, basically. So in a way, that's kind of true of Mountbatten. Yeah. You know, all these kind of posh guys in the 70s wanting to, a military takeover. General Swalter Walker, Davis Sterling,

144
01:13:02.000 --> 01:13:15.000
all of these people. Yeah. Yeah, they look back to the war or something and they say, well, that was brilliant. I wish we could have that back again. I said of all these grubby political compromises and Harold Wilson's nasty raincoats. All of this.

145
01:13:15.000 --> 01:14:01.000
So I think with Gusty Spence and a lot of people like this, and this goes, by the way, for people on the Republican side, for people in the IRA, there was a sense of a romantic history of sort of outlaw violence and adventure and whatnot. It's all about the songs and the fiddles and the stories and all of that kind of thing. And they are almost willing it back into being because at a time when, you know, jobs are scarce, life seems shabby. and disappointing, this would give you a sense of excitement. And of course, if you ally that to the fear of the others, then that becomes a very potent combination. The sense is that there is nothing inevitable about the troubles. People choose it.

146
01:14:01.000 --> 01:14:27.000
People choose it. As you say, they will it into existence. Yes, they absolutely do. And I think they, this search for a cause, and it's a cause that they're very familiar with is a really important part of the story. Spence said later, there was still a sense, like with the old Ulster volunteers that you were for the cause, you would fight and die for it. Yes, fight and die for the cause. And that's so widespread among the people who join the different

147
01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:57.000
paramilitary groups, whether loyalist or Republican. Fight and die and kill? Well, this is the thing. So do you remember when we did the series about the Irish War of Independence with Paul Rouse? He said all the ballads are about fighting and dying, but people don't write ballads about fighting and killing, but someone's got to do the killing. Yeah. And actually, this brings us very neatly to May 1966 and the first, arguably the first casualties of the troubles. At this point, Spence and his pals have already started petrol bombing, Catholic schools,

148
01:14:57.000 --> 01:15:23.000
Catholic shops and so on. In their minds, they are deterring IRA vandalism. This is their excuse. Of course, an external observer would say, this looks very much like a drunken, sectarian vandalism and bullying to me. On the 7th of May, they decide to target a Catholic-owned pub just off the Shankill Road. They say loads of Republicans hang out in this pub, which should blow this pub up. And they throw a petrol bomb through what they think is the landlord's window.

149
01:15:23.000 --> 01:15:49.000
But actually, it's the house next door. And it belongs to a Protestant grandmother called Matilda Gould, who was 77 years old. She was bedridden with severe arthritis. She can't get out of bed. She's horribly burned by this petrol bomb. and she dies seven weeks later of her injuries. And a lot of accounts point to her as the first victim, first modern victim of the troubles.

150
01:15:49.000 --> 01:16:14.000
And obviously this was a terrible mistake by the UVF to kill a Protestant grandmother. But in a sign of what's to come on both sides, they don't back down. They issue a statement two weeks later. From this day, we declare war against the IRA in its splinter groups. Known IRA men will be executed mercilessly and without hesitation, we solemnly warn the authorities to make no more speeches of appeasement.

151
01:16:14.000 --> 01:16:43.000
So there's two issues there. One is the sort of omnipresent, gnawing fear of the IRA that is there in loyalists' minds the whole time. But the IRA are a shadow at this point. They're very quiet at this point. I mean, a loyalist would say, come on, the IRA is still there, aren't they? And yes, they are still there, but or even though they're not actively campaigning. But the other thing is the attack on the authorities for appeasment. You know, this rhetoric runs right through the 60s and 70s.

152
01:16:43.000 --> 01:17:03.000
And on the 27th May, the UVF meet to discuss their next attack. They meet in a pub again. They said there's a bloke nearby called Leo Martin. He's a Republican activist. He's involved with the IRA. Let's go off and shoot him. And they go off to the Falls Road to go and find him and they can't find him. And on the way back, having not found him, they see walking down the street towards them,

153
01:17:03.000 --> 01:17:22.000
a bloke who's been in the pub all evening who's singing Republican songs he's called John Patrick Scullion he's 28 years old and they say well this bloke will do and they stab him and they shoot him and two weeks later he dies in hospital of his wounds and he's the second victim

154
01:17:22.000 --> 01:17:52.000
and then a month later a third victim the evening of Saturday the 25th of June there are four young Catholics four young blokes they've been working in Belfar City Centre working late, they decide to go for a last pint at the Moulven Arms just off the Shankill Road because they know it stays open after hours. This pub, the Moulven Arms, is a Protestant pub, but one of these bloke says, it's fine, I know the landlord, this guy's called Andrew Kelly, he says, okay, I know the landlord, hell of no problem with us going there. They get to the

155
01:17:52.000 --> 01:18:21.000
pub at about midnight, the pub is very busy, the landlord does know him, it's fine, they have no problem getting served, great. They sit down with their pints. Unknown to them, but sheer bad luck. The drinkers at the next table are gusty Spence and his gang. And one of the gangs said to the police later, the conversation came up about the religion of these fellows. Spence asked the company if they would be Catholics. Spence then went up to the bar beside the four lads to buy a drink.

156
01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:35.000
When he returned to our table, he said, I've been listening to their conversation, they're four IRA men. They're IRA men. They will have to go. Which they're not. They're not IRA men. They're absolutely not. The four Catholic, young Catholic guys have their pints.

157
01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:55.000
A couple of hours later, they leave the pub. And leading the way is one of them, Peter Ward, who is 18 years old. And as soon as they get outside, the shooting starts. Andrew Kelly again. He was dead, Peter Ward, when he hit the ground. I was shot too and I dropped down, but they kept on shooting at me. There were four gunmen. They didn't say anything.

158
01:18:55.000 --> 01:19:14.000
All I remember is the flashes and the shooting. So one of them, this guy Peter Ward has killed. Spence and his gang were arrested and Spence and two others were sent to prison. Now, Paisley always condemns shootings of this kind. He's very happy to ratchet up the tension

159
01:19:14.000 --> 01:19:33.000
rhetorically, but he almost always distances himself from actual violence. And that again is something you, you know, he's not unusual in that regard. There are loads of people in their story who are very happy to kind of stoke the flames, but when the bullets fly, They always say, oh, no, I don't agree with this, of course.

160
01:19:33.000 --> 01:20:02.000
Do you think that's a cynical take, or is he acting on genuine principle when he condemns it? That is, I think, something that only he would know. That only he would know, if you're asking me my personal verdict, which not all thisers would agree with, I would say, I think there's an awful amount of cynicism from people in this story. So I'm not just talking about Ian Paisley, I'm talking about Jerry Adams or whoever. there are an awful lot of people who say, I don't really support violence, I don't really encourage it,

161
01:20:02.000 --> 01:20:17.000
but everything that they do seems designed to encourage it because they believe it's in their political interests, frankly. Okay, okay. So, yeah, I would take a very caustic view of such people personally. And actually, to go back, just to finish on, Spence & Co,

162
01:20:17.000 --> 01:20:34.000
these killings were just so terrible to us. To some people, they give them a kind of outlaw appeal. They seem exciting. One writer later called Spencer Loyalist Folk Hero and there are lots of loyalist gunmen who joined paramountry who said yes

163
01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:56.000
he was my inspiration I really looked up to this guy he was the one person who had the guts to take up the gun and to actually do something positive to defend our people so I think that that's an important thing to bear in mind in Britain what do people think of all this do you know what most people don't even notice this is the summer of swinging London It's the summer of England's World Cup win.

164
01:20:56.000 --> 01:21:08.000
Most people are completely uninterested in Northern Ireland. It doesn't really fit the mood these stories. Who cares that some people in a faraway place have been shooting each other if all your thoughts

165
01:21:08.000 --> 01:21:17.000
are of Carnaby Street and mini skirts and stuff. But of course in Belfast, people don't think that way at all. Terence O'Neill had been on a visit to France for the anniversary of the Somme and he cuts it short

166
01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:38.000
to come back to Stormont and to denounce the Ulster Volunteer Force. He says, it's abominable to compare yourselves to Carson and his volunteers. There's no connection between men who are ready to die for the country and the fields of France and this sordid conspiracy of criminals. And he ends with this really dramatic warning, O'Neill. He says,

167
01:21:38.000 --> 01:22:01.000
Northern Ireland must choose either the road to progress or return to the pointless violence of the past. When he gives the speech, the London press say, Oh, this is great. O'Neill is laying down the gauntlet. and people will undoubtedly rally to him because people don't want to go back to the sectarian hatreds of the past. But the biggest newspaper in Belfast,

168
01:22:01.000 --> 01:22:20.000
the Belfast Telegraph, which has a much better sense of the local mood, is much less optimistic. It says Ulster is in danger of being thrown back into a dark past thanks to those who have been sewing dragons teeth and do not care how terrible the harvest may be. But Tom, that harvest will be much more terrible

169
01:22:20.000 --> 01:23:04.000
than anybody realises. So next time we'll be looking at the civil rights marches of 1968, the fighting in the streets of Derry, your hero, Jim Callahan, deciding to send in the troops after a lot of prevarication and the IRA preparing to reemerge from the shadows. So club members can hear that episode and the entire series. And if you're not a member, you can, of course, get members. at the rest is history.com. And as we may have mentioned before, membership does come with an incredible rate of supplementary benefit, including our fantastic newsletter,

